      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">How Hanoi parking actually works</h2>
    <p>Most parking in central Hanoi is organised, attended and cheap or free. Side-street lots, building basements, cordoned pavement sections with a minder and a stack of tickets: these are the norm, not the exception. The attendant system is the single biggest ally a renting pair has, because a bike inside a supervised lot is a bike someone is paid to notice. The strategy that follows leans on this system deliberately instead of treating it as a last resort.</p>
    <p>The exceptions matter just as much. Alleys that look like parking may be somebody's front room; a gap between two bikes on a slope may be the reason both of them are scratched; a stretch of pavement may belong to a shop that will move anything blocking its door. When in doubt, the rule of thumb is simple: if there is no obvious place a local would leave a bike, there is no place for a rental either.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">The three questions to ask at any destination</h2>
    <p>Before leaving the bike anywhere for more than a minute, a pair should be able to answer three questions. First: does anyone here watch the bikes? Second: when does this spot stop being open, meaning does the lot close, does the street clear for market hours, does the pavement flood in the afternoon? Third: could I find this spot again in the dark, on foot, tired? A spot that fails any of the three is not a spot, it is a bet.</p>
    <p>Attendants usually solve the first two questions instantly, and the third is solved by a photograph taken before walking away: the bike, its surroundings, and the nearest corner with a street sign. The photo takes four seconds and has ended more confused searches than any other habit in this page. It also settles the occasional honest disagreement between rider and passenger about which side street they turned in on.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Curbs, sidewalks and the unwritten rules</h2>
    <p>Pavement parking is everywhere in Hanoi, and most of it follows a silent logic. Motorbikes park perpendicular to the kerb in tight rows, front wheels to the wall, leaving a walking lane. Joining an existing row at its end is welcome; inventing a new row across a doorway or a fire exit is not. If the pavement has a painted line, a rope, a stack of boards or any sign of arrangement, an attendant is implied and a small fee may be expected.</p>
    <p>For a loaded pair, the extra care points are the stand and the lean. Park with the bike facing out so it can be pushed rather than reversed into traffic. Test the side stand on the slope before committing, and if the slope is wrong, turn the rear wheel to the kerb. On wet pavement, stands sink and bikes fall, so a few flat stones or a firm patch matter more than proximity. The machine checks that prevent a bad surprise are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-luggage-pre-ride-inspection/">pre-ride inspection page</a>, and the same checks run in reverse before leaving a bike for hours.</p>
    <p>Two more details complete the picture. Rain shelters and awnings change the rules: a spot that is legal in dry weather can be under water in a squall, and drainage dips at the low edge of every pavement. And restaurant hours matter more than riders expect, because a bike parked against a shutter at night belongs to whatever opens first in the morning; parking across a business's access is the classic way to find a bike moved, scratched or boxed in.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Securing the bike and the two helmets</h2>
    <p>Locking discipline is about raising effort, not achieving perfection. Use the bike's steering lock every single time, plus a cable or chain through the rear wheel or around a post where one exists. Two-up means two helmets, and helmets are the most commonly lost item of any rental: keep both with you in a bag, clip them to the bike under the seat where the design allows, or hand them to the attendant, who will hang them on a peg and remember. Choose one habit and keep it for the whole rental.</p>
    <p>Belongings follow the same logic. A pair carries more, so the rule is that nothing that matters rides in an unwatched top box overnight, and the day's bag goes with the people, not with the bike. The paperwork questions that matter at pickup, including what the rental covers and what it does not, are worth re-reading on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/motorbike-rental-deposit-hanoi/">deposit page</a> so that a parking loss never becomes a surprise deduction.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Overnight parking for pairs</h2>
    <p>Overnight is the highest-risk window of any rental, and the strategy is to buy supervision rather than trust luck. Hostels and hotels usually have a garage or an arrangement with a nearby lot; ask at check-in, and if the answer is a shrug, find the nearest attended lot yourself and ask the price for a night. A bike that sleeps indoors behind a ticket beats a bike that sleeps on an open street every time, whatever the cost difference turns out to be.</p>
    <p>For a two-up day that ends late, plan the overnight spot while riding, not after arriving tired. Note where the attended lots were on the way in, and prefer them even when the hotel offers a dark corner of the forecourt. The companion pages in this cluster, like the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-luggage-parking-strategy/">parking strategy for riders with luggage</a>, cover the same trade-offs for riders carrying bags instead of people, and the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how-to-rent guide</a> explains what to sort at the counter before any of this applies.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">What to do if the bike is not where you left it</h2>
    <p>First, check the obvious. Attendants move bikes constantly to make room, so walk the whole lot before panicking, and ask the minder with the photograph. Bikes are also ticketed for bad parking and relocated to the other side of the street or around the corner by the people who manage the pavement; a lap of the block resolves most disappearances in ten minutes.</p>
    <p>If the bike is genuinely gone, the order of operations is: contact the rental shop, then the police report if the shop asks for one, then your insurer if you have travel insurance that covers rentals. The shop needs the rental details, the location and the time, which is exactly why the pickup photographs and paperwork from day one matter. Do not pay anyone who appears claiming to know where the bike is; that conversation has only one ending.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">Building the parking plan into the day</h2>
    <p>Good parking is a routing decision. When a pair plans a day around destinations, the plan should include one candidate parking spot per stop, chosen on the map before setting out. Busy destinations like the Old Quarter at dinner time reward arriving early or parking two streets out and walking five minutes. The walk costs nothing; the third lap of a full street in the dark with a passenger waiting behind costs the whole evening.</p>
    <p>The habit that binds the page together is the two-minute routine at every stop: photograph the spot, lock the steering, decide the helmets, agree a meeting point if the pair separates. Two minutes, every stop, no exceptions. Pairs who keep the routine finish rentals without a single parking story worth telling, which in the parking chapter is exactly the goal.</p>

    <section class="faq">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>Is it safe to park a rental motorbike on the street in Hanoi?</h3>
        <p>Attended lots and supervised pavement sections are the safer default and the local norm. Open street parking overnight is the highest-risk option and is best avoided, especially for a bike carrying two helmets and a pair's belongings.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>Do I have to pay for parking in Hanoi?</h3>
        <p>Many attended lots and pavement sections charge a small fee, and some are free. This page does not quote figures; ask the attendant before leaving the bike, and expect loose change to settle it.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>What should I do with two helmets when parking?</h3>
        <p>Carry them, clip them to the bike where the design allows, or hand them to the attendant. Pick one habit and repeat it at every stop so helmets never depend on memory.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>How do I not lose the bike in a big lot?</h3>
        <p>Take a photo of the parked bike with the nearest street sign or landmark in frame before walking away. It also helps to park facing out so the machine is easy to push into the flow when leaving.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>What if the rental bike disappears?</h3>
        <p>Search the lot and the block first, ask the attendant with your photo, then contact the rental shop. Keep the rental paperwork and pickup photos from day one; they are what the shop will ask for first.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>Should we park close to the entrance of a busy destination?</h3>
        <p>Not necessarily. The closest spots are the most contested and the most likely to be rearranged. Parking two streets out in an attended lot and walking five minutes usually returns the bike in the same condition it was left in.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="faq-entry">
        <h3>Does riding with a passenger change how we park?</h3>
        <p>Mainly through weight and bulk. A loaded bike tips more easily on slopes and soft ground, so the stand check matters more, and two helmets mean the carry-away routine is longer. Build the extra minute into the plan.</p>
      </article>
    </section>
